// --- Lesson 1: End of WWII and Origins of the Cold War ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch1_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## The End of World War II and the Origins of the Cold War\n\nThe Cold War was a period of intense political, ideological, and military tension between the **United States of America (USA)** and the **Union of Soviet Socialist Republics (USSR)** that lasted from approximately **1947 to 1991**. Although the two superpowers never engaged in direct military conflict with each other, they fought proxy wars, engaged in an arms race, and competed for global influence.\n\n### The Wartime Alliance\n\nDuring World War II (1939\u20131945), the USA, Britain, and the USSR were allies against Nazi Germany. However, this alliance was one of convenience rather than shared values:\n- The **USA and Britain** were capitalist democracies that believed in free elections, private ownership, and individual liberties.\n- The **USSR** was a communist state under Joseph Stalin, where the state controlled the economy and political opposition was suppressed.'),
  t(2, '### Key Conferences and Growing Tensions\n\n| Conference | Date | Key Decisions |\n|-----------|------|---------------|\n| **Yalta** | February 1945 | Germany to be divided into four zones; free elections in Eastern Europe; USSR to join war against Japan |\n| **Potsdam** | July\u2013August 1945 | Confirmed division of Germany; disagreements over reparations; Truman informed Stalin of the atomic bomb |\n\n**Why did the Cold War develop?**\n\n1. **Ideological differences**: Capitalism vs Communism \u2014 two fundamentally incompatible systems of government and economics\n2. **Mutual suspicion**: The West feared Soviet expansion; the USSR feared Western aggression and encirclement\n3. **Broken promises**: Stalin did not hold free elections in Eastern Europe as agreed at Yalta\n4. **The atomic bomb**: The US monopoly on nuclear weapons (1945\u20131949) made the Soviets feel threatened\n5. **Power vacuum**: The defeat of Germany and Japan left a power vacuum that both superpowers sought to fill'),
  q(3, 'Which conference saw the Western Allies and the USSR agree that Germany would be divided into four occupation zones?',
    ['Yalta (February 1945)', 'Potsdam (July 1945)', 'Tehran (November 1943)', 'San Francisco (April 1945)'], 0,
    'At the Yalta Conference in February 1945, the Big Three (Roosevelt, Churchill, Stalin) agreed that Germany would be divided into four zones of occupation \u2014 American, British, French, and Soviet.'),
  t(4, '### The Creation of Spheres of Interest (Satellite States)\n\nBetween 1945 and 1948, the Soviet Union established communist governments in the countries of Eastern Europe that the Red Army had liberated from Nazi control. These became known as **satellite states** because they were technically independent but in reality were controlled by Moscow.\n\n**Soviet satellite states included:**\n- Poland (1947)\n- Czechoslovakia (1948)\n- Hungary (1947)\n- Romania (1947)\n- Bulgaria (1946)\n- East Germany (1949)\n- Yugoslavia (under Tito, broke with Moscow in 1948)\n\nWinston Churchill described this division in his famous **"Iron Curtain" speech** (March 1946, Fulton, Missouri): *"From Stettin in the Baltic to Trieste in the Adriatic, an iron curtain has descended across the continent."*\n\nThis speech is seen by many historians as one of the opening salvos of the Cold War.'),
  fb(5, 'The term "___ Curtain" was used by Winston Churchill in 1946 to describe the division between Western and Eastern Europe. Countries controlled by the USSR were known as ___ states.',
    ['Iron', 'satellite'],
    'Churchill used "Iron Curtain" in his famous speech at Fulton, Missouri. Eastern European countries under Soviet control were called satellite states.'),
  t(6, '### US Policy of Containment\n\nIn response to Soviet expansion, the USA adopted a policy of **containment** \u2014 preventing the spread of communism without directly attacking the USSR.\n\n**The Truman Doctrine (March 1947)**\n- President Harry Truman declared that the USA would support free peoples resisting subjugation by armed minorities or outside pressures\n- Immediate context: Greece and Turkey were under threat from communist insurgents\n- The USA provided $400 million in military and economic aid\n- Significance: Marked the official start of US involvement in containing communism worldwide\n\n**The Marshall Plan (June 1947)**\n- Secretary of State George Marshall proposed a massive programme of economic aid to rebuild war-torn Europe\n- Between 1948 and 1952, the USA provided approximately **$13 billion** (over $150 billion in today\u2019s money)\n- Aim: Rebuild European economies so that poverty and despair would not drive people towards communism\n- The USSR rejected Marshall Plan aid and forced its satellite states to do the same\n- Stalin saw the Marshall Plan as an attempt to buy European loyalty and extend American economic dominance'),
  q(7, 'What was the primary purpose of the Marshall Plan?',
    ['To rebuild European economies and prevent the spread of communism', 'To create a military alliance against the USSR', 'To divide Germany into occupation zones', 'To provide nuclear weapons to Western Europe'], 0,
    'The Marshall Plan (1947) provided economic aid to rebuild Europe. The US believed that economic recovery would create stability and prevent communist movements from gaining support among desperate, impoverished populations.'),
  t(8, '### The Berlin Crisis (1948\u20131949) and the Berlin Wall (1961)\n\n**The Berlin Blockade (1948\u20131949)**\n- Berlin was deep inside the Soviet zone but divided into four sectors\n- In June 1948, the Western powers introduced a new currency (the Deutschmark) in their zones, angering Stalin\n- Stalin blockaded all road, rail, and canal access to West Berlin, hoping to force the West out\n- The USA and Britain responded with the **Berlin Airlift**: for 11 months (June 1948 \u2013 May 1949), they flew supplies into West Berlin\n- Over 275,000 flights delivered 2.3 million tonnes of supplies\n- Stalin lifted the blockade in May 1949 \u2014 a significant propaganda defeat for the USSR\n\n**The Berlin Wall (1961)**\n- By 1961, approximately 3.5 million East Germans had fled to the West through Berlin\n- On **13 August 1961**, East Germany began building a wall dividing East and West Berlin\n- The wall became the most powerful symbol of the Cold War\n- Hundreds of people were killed trying to cross it'),
  q(9, 'How did the Western powers respond to the Soviet blockade of West Berlin in 1948?',
    ['They organised a massive airlift to supply West Berlin', 'They sent tanks to break through the blockade', 'They agreed to leave Berlin', 'They launched a nuclear strike'], 0,
    'The Berlin Airlift (June 1948 \u2013 May 1949) was the Western response. Over 275,000 flights delivered food, fuel, and supplies to the 2 million people of West Berlin without provoking direct military confrontation.'),
  fb(10, 'NATO was established in ___ as a Western military alliance. The Soviet response was the ___ Pact, established in 1955.',
    ['1949', 'Warsaw'],
    'NATO (North Atlantic Treaty Organization) was founded in 1949. The Warsaw Pact was the Soviet-led military alliance created in 1955 as a direct response to West Germany joining NATO.'),
  t(11, '### NATO and the Warsaw Pact\n\n**NATO (North Atlantic Treaty Organization) \u2014 1949**\n- Military alliance of Western nations\n- Principle of collective security: an attack on one member is an attack on all\n- Original members included the USA, Britain, France, Canada, and others\n- West Germany joined in 1955\n\n**Warsaw Pact \u2014 1955**\n- Military alliance of the USSR and its satellite states\n- Created in direct response to West Germany joining NATO\n- Members: USSR, Poland, East Germany, Czechoslovakia, Hungary, Romania, Bulgaria, Albania\n\n### Who Was to Blame for the Cold War?\n\n| Perspective | Argument |\n|------------|----------|\n| **Orthodox (Western) view** | The USSR was to blame \u2014 Stalin\u2019s aggressive expansion into Eastern Europe violated agreements |\n| **Revisionist view** | The USA was to blame \u2014 the atomic bomb, Marshall Plan, and NATO were provocative |\n| **Post-revisionist view** | Both sides shared responsibility \u2014 mutual suspicion and misunderstanding escalated tensions |'),
  q(12, 'Which historical perspective argues that BOTH superpowers shared responsibility for the Cold War?',
    ['Post-revisionist', 'Orthodox', 'Revisionist', 'Marxist'], 0,
    'The post-revisionist view, which emerged from the 1970s onwards, argues that both the USA and USSR contributed to Cold War tensions through mutual suspicion, misperception, and reactive policies.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Cold War Case Study \u2014 Vietnam ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch1_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Cold War Case Study: The Vietnam War (1957\u20131975)\n\nVietnam is a crucial case study of the Cold War in action. It demonstrates how superpower rivalry was played out in a developing country, with devastating consequences for its people.\n\n### Background\n\n- Vietnam was a French colony (part of French Indochina) until 1954\n- In 1954, the Viet Minh (Vietnamese communist nationalists) defeated France at the **Battle of Dien Bien Phu**\n- The **Geneva Accords (1954)** temporarily divided Vietnam at the 17th parallel:\n  - **North Vietnam**: Communist, led by **Ho Chi Minh**, supported by the USSR and China\n  - **South Vietnam**: Non-communist, led by **Ngo Dinh Diem**, supported by the USA\n- Reunification elections were promised but never held \u2014 the US feared Ho Chi Minh would win\n\n### The Domino Theory\n\nThe US justified its involvement using the **Domino Theory**: if one country fell to communism, neighbouring countries would follow like a row of dominoes. Vietnam was seen as a crucial test case.'),
  t(2, '### The Struggle Between South Vietnam and the Viet Cong (1957\u20131963)\n\n- The **Viet Cong** (National Liberation Front) was a communist guerrilla movement in South Vietnam, supported by North Vietnam\n- They used **guerrilla warfare**: ambushes, booby traps, hit-and-run attacks, and tunnels (notably the **Cu Chi tunnels**)\n- The Viet Cong were difficult to distinguish from ordinary South Vietnamese civilians\n- The South Vietnamese government under Diem was **corrupt and unpopular** \u2014 he persecuted Buddhists and political opponents\n- Buddhist monks set themselves on fire in protest (self-immolation)\n- In **November 1963**, Diem was overthrown and assassinated in a military coup (the CIA was aware of the plot)\n\n### US Involvement Escalates (1965\u20131969)\n\n- In August 1964, the **Gulf of Tonkin incident** gave President Lyndon B. Johnson the pretext to escalate\n- Congress passed the **Gulf of Tonkin Resolution**, giving Johnson authority to use military force\n- By 1968, over **500,000 US troops** were in Vietnam'),
  q(3, 'What was the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution (1964)?',
    ['It gave President Johnson authority to use military force in Vietnam', 'It divided Vietnam at the 17th parallel', 'It ended the French colonial presence in Vietnam', 'It created a peace agreement between North and South Vietnam'], 0,
    'The Gulf of Tonkin Resolution (August 1964) was passed by Congress after alleged North Vietnamese attacks on US naval vessels. It effectively gave President Johnson a blank cheque to escalate US military involvement without a formal declaration of war.'),
  t(4, '### US Military Tactics\n\n| Tactic | Description | Impact |\n|--------|-------------|--------|\n| **Operation Rolling Thunder** | Sustained bombing campaign of North Vietnam (1965\u20131968) | Over 600,000 tonnes of bombs dropped; failed to break North Vietnamese will |\n| **Search and Destroy** | Ground operations to find and eliminate Viet Cong | Soldiers struggled to identify the enemy; led to civilian casualties |\n| **Agent Orange** | Chemical defoliant sprayed to destroy jungle cover | Over 20 million gallons sprayed; caused cancer and birth defects in Vietnamese and US soldiers |\n| **Napalm** | Incendiary gel bombs | Caused horrific burns; iconic images turned public opinion |\n| **Strategic hamlets** | Forcibly relocating villagers to deny Viet Cong support | Deeply unpopular; often pushed villagers towards the Viet Cong |'),
  t(5, '### The Tet Offensive (January 1968)\n\nThe **Tet Offensive** was a turning point in the war:\n- During the Vietnamese New Year (Tet), the Viet Cong and North Vietnamese Army launched coordinated attacks on over **100 cities and towns** in South Vietnam\n- They even briefly seized the US Embassy compound in Saigon\n- Militarily, the offensive was a failure \u2014 the communists suffered heavy casualties and were pushed back\n- **Politically, it was a massive success**: it shattered the US government\u2019s claims that the war was being won\n- American television viewers saw the reality of the war in their living rooms\n- Public support for the war collapsed\n- President Johnson announced he would **not seek re-election** in March 1968'),
  q(6, 'Why was the Tet Offensive (1968) considered a political success for the Viet Cong despite being a military failure?',
    ['It shattered US public confidence that the war was being won', 'It captured the South Vietnamese capital permanently', 'It forced the immediate withdrawal of US troops', 'It convinced China to send troops to support North Vietnam'], 0,
    'The Tet Offensive showed the American public that despite years of war and government assurances of progress, the enemy was capable of launching a massive coordinated attack. Television coverage of the fighting brought the brutal reality of the war into American homes.'),
  t(7, '### US Withdrawal (1969\u20131975)\n\n**Vietnamisation (from 1969)**\n- President Richard Nixon began a policy of **Vietnamisation**: gradually withdrawing US troops while training and equipping the South Vietnamese army (ARVN) to fight on its own\n- US troop numbers fell from 500,000 (1968) to 69,000 (1972)\n\n**The Paris Peace Accords (January 1973)**\n- Signed by the USA, North Vietnam, South Vietnam, and the Viet Cong\n- Ceasefire agreement; US troops to withdraw within 60 days\n- North Vietnamese troops were allowed to remain in South Vietnam\n\n**The Fall of Saigon (30 April 1975)**\n- Without US support, South Vietnam collapsed\n- North Vietnamese forces captured Saigon\n- The last Americans were evacuated by helicopter from the US Embassy roof \u2014 one of the most iconic images of the 20th century\n- Vietnam was reunified as a **communist state**'),
  fb(8, 'Nixon\u2019s policy of gradually withdrawing US troops while training the South Vietnamese army was called ___. The war ended with the Fall of ___ on 30 April 1975.',
    ['Vietnamisation', 'Saigon'],
    'Vietnamisation was Nixon\u2019s strategy from 1969 to transfer responsibility for the war to South Vietnam. The Fall of Saigon in April 1975 marked the final defeat of South Vietnam.'),
  t(9, '### Impact of the Vietnam War on US Domestic Politics\n\n1. **Anti-war movement**: Massive protests across America, especially on university campuses; Kent State shootings (1970) where National Guard killed 4 student protesters\n2. **The credibility gap**: The public lost trust in government claims about the war\n3. **The Pentagon Papers (1971)**: Leaked classified documents showed the government had systematically lied about the war\n4. **Watergate**: Nixon\u2019s paranoia about leaks led to illegal surveillance, contributing to the Watergate scandal and his resignation in 1974\n5. **War Powers Act (1973)**: Congress limited the president\u2019s ability to commit troops without Congressional approval\n6. **Vietnam Syndrome**: A reluctance to commit US troops abroad that lasted decades\n7. **Human cost**: Over 58,000 Americans killed; an estimated 2\u20133 million Vietnamese killed; widespread PTSD among veterans\n8. **Draft resistance**: The draft (conscription) was deeply unpopular; many young men fled to Canada or burned their draft cards'),
  q(10, 'Which event involved leaked classified documents that revealed the US government had lied about the Vietnam War?',
    ['The Pentagon Papers (1971)', 'The Watergate scandal', 'The Gulf of Tonkin incident', 'The Tet Offensive'], 0,
    'The Pentagon Papers were classified documents leaked by Daniel Ellsberg to The New York Times in 1971. They revealed that the US government had systematically lied about the progress and nature of the war to the American public and Congress.'),
  t(11, '### Vietnam as a Global Issue\n\nThe Vietnam War had significance beyond the USA and Vietnam:\n- It was seen as a **Cold War proxy war** \u2014 the USSR and China supplied North Vietnam while the USA backed the South\n- It inspired **anti-war and anti-imperialist movements** worldwide\n- It demonstrated the limits of superpower military might against guerrilla warfare\n- It raised questions about **national self-determination** vs superpower intervention\n- In **South Africa**, the apartheid government supported the US position and feared communist expansion in southern Africa\n- The war influenced debates about SA\u2019s own involvement in Angola and the Border War'),
  q(12, 'How does the Vietnam War connect to South African history?',
    ['SA feared communist expansion in southern Africa and became involved in the Angolan conflict', 'SA sent troops to fight in Vietnam', 'Vietnam provided weapons to the ANC', 'There is no connection between Vietnam and South African history'], 0,
    'The apartheid government in SA supported the US anti-communist stance. The fear of communist expansion in southern Africa was a key factor in SA\u2019s military involvement in Angola and its border war, which will be studied in the next chapter.'),
];

