// --- Lesson 1: The Angolan Conflict ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch2_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Independent Africa in the Cold War: The Angolan Conflict\n\nAngola provides a powerful case study of how Cold War rivalries played out on the African continent. The involvement of the USA, USSR, Cuba, and South Africa turned a post-colonial struggle into an internationalised conflict that lasted decades.\n\n### Colonialism and Independence\n\n- Angola was a **Portuguese colony** for over 400 years (from the 1480s)\n- Portugal was one of the last European powers to hold on to its African colonies\n- Three liberation movements emerged, each with different ethnic bases, ideologies, and foreign backers:\n\n| Movement | Leader | Ideology | Foreign Support |\n|----------|--------|----------|----------------|\n| **MPLA** (People\u2019s Movement for the Liberation of Angola) | Agostinho Neto | Marxist-Leninist | USSR, Cuba |\n| **FNLA** (National Front for the Liberation of Angola) | Holden Roberto | Pro-Western | USA, Za\u00efre (Congo) |\n| **UNITA** (National Union for the Total Independence of Angola) | Jonas Savimbi | Pro-Western (later Maoist) | USA, South Africa |\n\n- In **April 1974**, a military coup in Portugal (the **Carnation Revolution**) overthrew the dictatorship\n- The new Portuguese government agreed to grant independence to its colonies\n- Angola\u2019s independence was set for **11 November 1975**'),
  q(2, 'What event in Portugal led directly to Angola gaining independence?',
    ['The Carnation Revolution (April 1974)', 'The end of World War II', 'A United Nations resolution', 'Defeat of Portugal by the MPLA'], 0,
    'The Carnation Revolution of April 1974 was a military coup in Portugal that overthrew the authoritarian Estado Novo regime. The new government moved to end Portugal\u2019s colonial wars and granted independence to its African colonies.'),
  t(3, '### The Outbreak of Civil War (1974\u20131975)\n\nAs independence approached, the three liberation movements could not agree on a power-sharing arrangement:\n- The **Alvor Agreement (January 1975)** attempted to create a transitional government with all three movements, but it collapsed within weeks\n- Fighting broke out between the MPLA, FNLA, and UNITA\n- Each group controlled different regions of Angola\n- On **11 November 1975**, the MPLA declared independence in the capital, Luanda, with Agostinho Neto as president\n- The FNLA and UNITA declared their own rival governments\n\n### International Involvement\n\n**Why did foreign powers intervene?**\n- Angola\u2019s strategic importance: vast oil reserves, diamonds, and other minerals\n- Cold War logic: neither superpower wanted the other to gain a foothold\n- Regional dynamics: South Africa feared a Marxist state on its border; Cuba saw an opportunity to support a fellow socialist movement'),
  t(4, '### The Role of External Powers\n\n**USSR involvement:**\n- Provided weapons, military advisors, and funding to the MPLA from the 1960s\n- Massive arms shipments in 1975 as civil war intensified\n- Saw Angola as an opportunity to extend Soviet influence in Africa\n\n**Cuban involvement:**\n- Cuba sent **thousands of combat troops** to support the MPLA (eventually over 50,000)\n- Fidel Castro portrayed Cuba\u2019s role as one of revolutionary solidarity with a fellow anti-colonial movement\n- Cuban troops played a decisive role in repelling the South African invasion in 1975\u20131976\n\n**US involvement:**\n- The CIA provided covert funding and weapons to the FNLA and UNITA\n- Used Za\u00efre (under Mobutu) as a conduit for weapons\n- Congress later passed the **Clark Amendment (1976)** prohibiting further covert aid to Angola\n- The amendment was repealed in 1985 under Reagan, who resumed support for UNITA\n\n**South African involvement:**\n- SA invaded Angola in **October 1975** (Operation Savannah) to support UNITA and prevent an MPLA victory\n- SA feared that a Marxist Angola would provide bases for SWAPO (fighting for Namibian independence) and the ANC\n- SA troops advanced deep into Angola but withdrew in early 1976 after failing to receive expected US support'),
  q(5, 'Which country sent the largest number of combat troops to support the MPLA in Angola?',
    ['Cuba', 'The Soviet Union', 'The United States', 'China'], 0,
    'Cuba sent over 50,000 combat troops to Angola in support of the MPLA. This was one of the largest military interventions by a developing country in another developing country during the Cold War.'),
  fb(6, 'The three Angolan liberation movements were the ___, the FNLA, and UNITA. South Africa invaded Angola in October 1975 in an operation codenamed Operation ___.',
    ['MPLA', 'Savannah'],
    'The MPLA (People\u2019s Movement for the Liberation of Angola), FNLA, and UNITA were the three main movements. SA\u2019s invasion was called Operation Savannah.'),
  t(7, '### The Battle of Cuito Cuanavale (1987\u20131988)\n\nThe **Battle of Cuito Cuanavale** was one of the largest conventional battles in Africa since World War II and a major turning point.\n\n**Context:**\n- By the late 1980s, South African forces (SADF) were supporting UNITA in southern Angola\n- The MPLA, backed by Cuban troops and Soviet weapons, launched an offensive against UNITA\n- SADF intervened to prevent UNITA\u2019s defeat near the town of **Cuito Cuanavale** in southeastern Angola\n\n**The Battle:**\n- Lasted from late 1987 to early 1988\n- SADF and UNITA forces besieged Cuito Cuanavale, held by FAPLA (Angolan army) and Cuban forces\n- Cuba dramatically increased its troop presence (to over 50,000) and deployed MiG-23 fighter jets\n- A military stalemate developed \u2014 neither side could achieve a decisive victory\n\n**Significance:**\n- The battle is interpreted differently by different sides:\n  - **Cuba and the MPLA** claim it as a great victory \u2014 they prevented the fall of Cuito Cuanavale\n  - **South Africa** claims it achieved its military objectives of stopping the FAPLA offensive\n  - In reality, it was a **strategic stalemate** that convinced all parties that a military solution was impossible'),
  t(8, '### Changing International Relationships and the Peace Process\n\nThe stalemate at Cuito Cuanavale, combined with broader changes in the Cold War, led to negotiations:\n\n**Factors driving the peace process:**\n1. **Military stalemate**: Neither side could win outright\n2. **Gorbachev\u2019s reforms**: The new Soviet leader was reducing USSR commitments abroad\n3. **Economic costs**: The war was financially draining for all parties, especially SA\n4. **International pressure**: The USA brokered negotiations (led by Chester Crocker)\n\n**The New York Accords (December 1988):**\n- Cuba agreed to withdraw its troops from Angola\n- South Africa agreed to withdraw from Angola and grant independence to **Namibia**\n- This was a landmark agreement linking Angolan and Namibian peace\n\n**Namibian independence** followed on **21 March 1990**\n\n**Legacy:**\n- Nelson Mandela called Cuito Cuanavale *"a turning point for the liberation of our continent and my people"*\n- The withdrawal of foreign forces did not end the Angolan Civil War \u2014 it continued until **2002** when Jonas Savimbi was killed\n- The conflict demonstrates how African countries became battlegrounds for Cold War rivalries'),
  q(9, 'What was agreed in the New York Accords of December 1988?',
    ['Cuba would withdraw from Angola and SA would grant Namibian independence', 'The MPLA and UNITA would share power', 'The USA would provide economic aid to Angola', 'South Africa would end apartheid'], 0,
    'The New York Accords (December 1988) linked the withdrawal of Cuban troops from Angola to South African withdrawal and Namibian independence. This "linkage" strategy was brokered by US diplomat Chester Crocker.'),
  fb(10, 'Nelson Mandela described the Battle of ___ as "a turning point for the liberation of our continent and my people." Namibia gained independence on 21 March ___.',
    ['Cuito Cuanavale', '1990'],
    'Mandela saw the outcome at Cuito Cuanavale as crucial for ending apartheid and achieving liberation across southern Africa. Namibia became independent on 21 March 1990.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: The Congo Case Study ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch2_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Independent Africa: The Congo Case Study\n\nThe Congo provides an important case study of the challenges facing newly independent African states during the Cold War era. It demonstrates how colonial legacies, Cold War interference, and leadership crises combined to produce instability.\n\n### Ideas That Influenced the Congo\n\n**Pan-Africanism:**\n- The belief in African unity and solidarity\n- Leaders like Kwame Nkrumah (Ghana) inspired Congolese nationalists\n- The All-African People\u2019s Conference in Accra (1958) energised independence movements\n\n**African nationalism:**\n- The desire for self-determination and freedom from colonial rule\n- Influenced by the broader wave of decolonisation sweeping Africa in the 1950s and 1960s\n\n**Cold War ideologies:**\n- Both capitalism and communism competed for influence in newly independent African states\n- The Congo\u2019s vast mineral wealth made it a prize for both superpowers'),
  t(2, '### Political Leadership and the Legacy of Colonialism\n\n**Belgian colonial rule** was particularly exploitative:\n- The Congo was initially the personal property of King Leopold II (the **Congo Free State**, 1885\u20131908), notorious for brutality and forced labour\n- When Belgium took over in 1908, it did little to prepare the Congo for independence\n- At independence in 1960, the Congo had **fewer than 30 university graduates** among a population of 15 million\n- There were virtually no Congolese doctors, engineers, lawyers, or senior civil servants\n- The colonial administration had deliberately prevented the development of an educated African elite\n\n**Key political figures:**\n\n| Leader | Role | Political stance |\n|--------|------|------------------|\n| **Patrice Lumumba** | First Prime Minister | Pan-Africanist, nationalist, sought genuine independence |\n| **Joseph Kasavubu** | First President | More conservative, federalist |\n| **Moise Tshombe** | Leader of Katanga secession | Pro-Western, backed by Belgian mining interests |\n| **Mobutu Sese Seko** | Army chief, later dictator | Pro-Western, CIA-backed |'),
  q(3, 'Why was the Congo so poorly prepared for independence in 1960?',
    ['Belgium deliberately prevented the development of an educated Congolese elite', 'The Congolese people rejected Western education', 'The Congo had been independent for too short a time', 'The United Nations prevented education in the Congo'], 0,
    'Belgian colonial policy deliberately limited African education and barred Congolese people from senior positions. At independence in 1960, the Congo had fewer than 30 university graduates among 15 million people, creating a severe leadership and skills deficit.'),
  t(4, '### The Congo Crisis (1960\u20131965)\n\n**Independence (30 June 1960):**\n- Lumumba\u2019s speech at the independence ceremony was a powerful critique of colonialism: he spoke of the *"humiliating slavery that was imposed upon us by force"*\n- This angered the Belgians, who had expected a grateful, cooperative government\n\n**Immediate crises:**\n1. **Army mutiny** (July 1960): Congolese soldiers mutinied against their Belgian officers; violence against Belgian civilians\n2. **Katanga secession** (July 1960): The mineral-rich Katanga province, led by Tshombe and backed by Belgian mining company **Union Mini\u00e8re**, declared independence\n3. **Belgian military intervention**: Belgium sent troops, ostensibly to protect its citizens\n4. **UN intervention**: Lumumba appealed to the United Nations, which sent peacekeeping forces (ONUC)\n\n**Cold War intervention:**\n- Lumumba turned to the USSR for help when the UN and USA would not support him against Katanga\n- The USA and Belgium saw Lumumba as a communist threat\n- The **CIA authorised plans to assassinate Lumumba**\n- In September 1960, Colonel **Mobutu** (backed by the CIA) staged a coup\n- Lumumba was arrested, handed to his enemies in Katanga, and **murdered in January 1961**'),
  q(5, 'Why did Western powers view Patrice Lumumba as a threat?',
    ['He turned to the USSR for assistance when the West would not help against the Katanga secession', 'He refused to allow mining in the Congo', 'He attacked Belgian citizens', 'He was a communist party member'], 0,
    'Lumumba was not a communist, but his appeal to the USSR for help \u2014 after the West refused to support him against the Belgian-backed Katanga secession \u2014 made the USA view him as a potential Soviet ally. This Cold War lens led to CIA involvement in his overthrow and death.'),
  t(6, '### Type of Economy and Social/Cultural Challenges\n\n**Economic structure:**\n- The Congolese economy was based on **extractive industries**: copper, cobalt, diamonds, gold, uranium, and rubber\n- Most wealth flowed to Belgian and international companies, not to the Congolese people\n- The economy was heavily dependent on mining exports with little manufacturing or diversification\n- Infrastructure (roads, railways) was built to extract resources, not to connect communities\n\n**Social and cultural challenges:**\n- Over 200 ethnic groups speaking more than 200 languages\n- Colonial rule had exploited ethnic divisions through divide-and-rule policies\n- Rapid urbanisation without adequate services\n- High levels of poverty despite the country\u2019s vast mineral wealth (the "resource curse")\n- Limited healthcare and education systems'),
  t(7, '### Benefits of Independence, Education, and Africanisation\n\n**Benefits of independence:**\n- Political self-determination \u2014 the right of Congolese people to govern themselves\n- Freedom from the most extreme forms of colonial exploitation\n- A sense of national pride and African identity\n- Membership of international organisations (UN, OAU)\n\n**Education and Africanisation:**\n- After independence, there was a push to educate Congolese citizens for professional and government roles (**Africanisation**)\n- Universities were established (University of Kinshasa, University of Lubumbashi)\n- However, progress was slow due to lack of infrastructure and trained teachers\n- Under Mobutu, "Zairianisation" (from 1971) involved renaming the country **Za\u00efre** and promoting African culture\n- Mobutu\u2019s version of Africanisation was often superficial and authoritarian, serving to consolidate his power rather than genuinely empower citizens'),
  fb(8, 'The assassination of Patrice ___ in January 1961 was supported by the CIA and Belgian intelligence. The Congo was renamed ___ under Mobutu\u2019s rule.',
    ['Lumumba', 'Za\u00efre'],
    'Patrice Lumumba was the first democratically elected Prime Minister of the Congo. He was murdered in January 1961 with the complicity of the CIA and Belgian intelligence. Mobutu renamed the country Za\u00efre in 1971 as part of his Africanisation programme.'),
  q(9, 'What is meant by the "resource curse" in the context of the Congo?',
    ['A country rich in natural resources but with widespread poverty due to exploitation and corruption', 'A curse placed on the land by colonial powers', 'The exhaustion of the Congo\u2019s mineral reserves', 'Sanctions preventing the Congo from exporting minerals'], 0,
    'The "resource curse" describes the paradox where countries with abundant natural resources (like the Congo\u2019s copper, cobalt, and diamonds) often experience worse economic and political outcomes than resource-poor countries, due to corruption, foreign exploitation, and conflict over resource control.'),
  t(10, '### Source Analysis Skills: Evaluating Sources on the Congo\n\nWhen analysing sources about the Congo, consider:\n\n**Reliability:**\n- Who created the source? A Belgian official, a Congolese nationalist, a CIA operative?\n- When was it created? During the crisis or years later?\n- Does it have first-hand knowledge of events?\n\n**Usefulness:**\n- What information does the source provide about the topic?\n- Even a biased source can be useful \u2014 it reveals the perspective and motivations of its creator\n\n**Bias:**\n- Cold War sources are often heavily biased \u2014 Western sources may demonise Lumumba, Soviet sources may idealise him\n- Colonial sources often justified exploitation and presented Africans as incapable of self-governance\n- Always consider: What is the purpose of this source? Who was the intended audience?'),
];

