// --- Lesson 1: Origins and Martin Luther King Jr ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Civil Society Protests: The US Civil Rights Movement\n\n### Reasons and Origins\n\nThe Civil Rights Movement (1950s\u20131960s) was a mass struggle by African Americans to end racial segregation and discrimination in the United States.\n\n**Historical context:**\n- After the Civil War (1861\u20131865), slavery was abolished (13th Amendment, 1865)\n- The 14th Amendment (1868) granted citizenship to all persons born in the US\n- The 15th Amendment (1870) prohibited denying the vote based on race\n- Despite these amendments, **Jim Crow laws** (from the 1890s) enforced racial segregation in the South\n- The Supreme Court ruling in **Plessy v Ferguson (1896)** upheld "separate but equal" \u2014 legalising segregation\n- In reality, facilities for Black Americans were always inferior\n\n**Conditions under segregation:**\n- Separate and unequal schools, hospitals, buses, restaurants, water fountains, and toilets\n- Black Americans could not vote in many Southern states due to poll taxes, literacy tests, and intimidation\n- Lynching and violence were used to terrorise Black communities\n- Economic discrimination: Black Americans were restricted to low-paying jobs'),
  t(2, '### The Role of Martin Luther King Jr\n\nDr **Martin Luther King Jr** (1929\u20131968) was the most prominent leader of the Civil Rights Movement. He advocated **non-violent civil disobedience**, inspired by:\n- **Mahatma Gandhi\u2019s** philosophy of non-violent resistance (satyagraha) in India\n- Christian teachings on love and justice\n- Henry David Thoreau\u2019s essay "Civil Disobedience"\n\n**Key principles of King\u2019s approach:**\n1. Non-violent resistance to injustice\n2. Willingness to suffer without retaliation\n3. Appealing to the conscience of the oppressor\n4. Seeking to win friendship and understanding, not to defeat the opponent\n5. Love (agape) as a guiding force\n\nKing\u2019s famous **"I Have a Dream"** speech (28 August 1963) at the March on Washington articulated a vision of racial equality: *"I have a dream that my four little children will one day live in a nation where they will not be judged by the color of their skin but by the content of their character."*\n\nKing was awarded the **Nobel Peace Prize** in 1964. He was **assassinated** on 4 April 1968 in Memphis, Tennessee.'),
  q(3, 'Martin Luther King Jr\u2019s philosophy of non-violent resistance was primarily inspired by which figure?',
    ['Mahatma Gandhi', 'Malcolm X', 'Marcus Garvey', 'W.E.B. Du Bois'], 0,
    'King was deeply influenced by Gandhi\u2019s philosophy of non-violent resistance (satyagraha). He visited India in 1959 and studied Gandhi\u2019s methods of peaceful protest and civil disobedience, which he then applied to the American Civil Rights struggle.'),
  t(4, '### Forms of Protest: The Montgomery Bus Boycott (1955\u20131956)\n\nThe Montgomery Bus Boycott was one of the first major protests of the Civil Rights Movement:\n\n- On **1 December 1955**, **Rosa Parks** refused to give up her seat to a white passenger on a Montgomery, Alabama bus\n- Parks was arrested, sparking outrage in the Black community\n- The **Montgomery Improvement Association** was formed, with the young Martin Luther King Jr as its president\n- African Americans boycotted the city\u2019s buses for **381 days**\n- They walked, carpooled, and used Black-owned taxis\n- The boycott caused serious financial losses for the bus company\n- In November 1956, the Supreme Court ruled that bus segregation was **unconstitutional** (Browder v Gayle)\n- The boycott demonstrated the power of organised, non-violent mass action and economic pressure'),
  q(5, 'How long did the Montgomery Bus Boycott last?',
    ['381 days', '30 days', '100 days', '1 year exactly'], 0,
    'The Montgomery Bus Boycott lasted 381 days (December 1955 \u2013 December 1956), ending when the Supreme Court ruled that bus segregation was unconstitutional.'),
  t(6, '### Sit-ins and the Greensboro Campaign (1960)\n\nOn **1 February 1960**, four Black college students sat at a whites-only lunch counter at Woolworth\u2019s in **Greensboro, North Carolina**. They were refused service but remained seated.\n\n- Over the following days and weeks, hundreds of students joined the sit-ins\n- The movement spread to over **70 cities** across the South\n- Students endured verbal abuse, physical attacks, and arrest without retaliating\n- The **Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee (SNCC)** was formed in April 1960\n- By July 1960, Woolworth\u2019s desegregated its lunch counters\n\n**Freedom Rides (1961):**\n- Interracial groups rode buses through the South to test desegregation of interstate travel\n- They were met with extreme violence \u2014 buses were firebombed, riders were beaten\n- The violence generated national attention and forced the federal government to enforce desegregation'),
  fb(7, 'The sit-in movement began in February 1960 in ___, North Carolina. The student organisation formed to coordinate these protests was called the ___ (SNCC).',
    ['Greensboro', 'Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee'],
    'The Greensboro sit-ins at the Woolworth\u2019s lunch counter inspired a nationwide sit-in movement. SNCC was formed in April 1960 to coordinate student activism.'),
  t(8, '### The Birmingham Campaign (1963)\n\nBirmingham, Alabama was one of the most segregated cities in America. King and the SCLC (Southern Christian Leadership Conference) targeted it deliberately.\n\n**Key events:**\n- Mass marches, sit-ins, and boycotts of white-owned businesses\n- Police Commissioner **Eugene "Bull" Connor** used fire hoses, police dogs, and mass arrests against peaceful protesters, including **children** (the "Children\u2019s Crusade")\n- Television images of children being attacked by dogs and blasted with high-pressure hoses shocked the nation and the world\n- Over **2,500 people** were arrested\n- King wrote his famous **"Letter from Birmingham Jail"** (April 1963), defending non-violent direct action\n\n**Outcomes:**\n- Birmingham\u2019s businesses agreed to desegregate\n- President **John F. Kennedy** was moved to propose comprehensive civil rights legislation\n- The 16th Street Baptist Church bombing (September 1963) killed four young girls, highlighting the dangers Black Americans faced'),
  q(9, 'Why was the Birmingham Campaign (1963) a turning point for the Civil Rights Movement?',
    ['Television images of police brutality against peaceful protesters, including children, shocked the nation', 'It was the first successful desegregation campaign', 'Martin Luther King Jr was released from prison', 'The Supreme Court declared all segregation unconstitutional'], 0,
    'The brutal response of Bull Connor\u2019s police force \u2014 using dogs and fire hoses against peaceful demonstrators, including children \u2014 was broadcast on national television. These images galvanised public support for civil rights and pressured President Kennedy to act.'),
  t(10, '### Selma to Montgomery Marches (1965) and School Desegregation\n\n**Selma to Montgomery Marches (March 1965):**\n- Three marches were organised to demand voting rights\n- On **"Bloody Sunday"** (7 March 1965), state troopers attacked marchers at the Edmund Pettus Bridge with tear gas and clubs\n- Television coverage of the violence generated national outrage\n- King led a successful third march with federal protection\n- This directly led to the **Voting Rights Act of 1965**, which outlawed discriminatory voting practices\n\n**School Desegregation \u2014 Little Rock, Arkansas (1957):**\n- In 1954, the Supreme Court ruled in **Brown v Board of Education** that school segregation was unconstitutional\n- In 1957, nine Black students (the **"Little Rock Nine"**) attempted to attend Central High School in Little Rock, Arkansas\n- Governor Orval Faubus used the National Guard to block them\n- President **Eisenhower** sent federal troops to escort the students into the school\n- This demonstrated that the federal government would enforce desegregation, even against state resistance'),
  fb(11, 'The Voting Rights Act was passed in ___ following the Selma to Montgomery marches. The Supreme Court case that declared school segregation unconstitutional was ___ v Board of Education (1954).',
    ['1965', 'Brown'],
    'The Voting Rights Act of 1965 was a direct result of the Selma marches and the national outrage at Bloody Sunday. Brown v Board of Education (1954) overturned the "separate but equal" doctrine of Plessy v Ferguson.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: The Black Power Movement ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## The Black Power Movement\n\n### Reasons for the Rise of Black Power\n\nBy the mid-1960s, many younger African Americans were frustrated with the pace of change and the limitations of non-violent protest:\n\n1. **Continued violence**: Despite civil rights legislation, Black people were still being attacked and killed\n2. **Northern ghettos**: The Civil Rights Movement had focused on the South, but Black people in Northern cities faced poverty, police brutality, and de facto segregation\n3. **Economic inequality**: Legal desegregation did not address deep economic disparities\n4. **Watts Riots (1965)**: A police incident in the Watts neighbourhood of Los Angeles sparked six days of rioting, reflecting the anger of urban Black communities\n5. **Influence of African independence movements**: Decolonisation in Africa inspired pride in Black identity\n6. **Frustration with gradualism**: Some felt that King\u2019s approach was too slow and accommodating'),
  t(2, '### Malcolm X (1925\u20131965)\n\n**Malcolm X** was one of the most influential voices of the Black Power era:\n\n- Born Malcolm Little, he converted to Islam in prison and joined the **Nation of Islam** (NOI), led by Elijah Muhammad\n- He took the surname "X" to represent his lost African family name\n- Unlike King, Malcolm X advocated **self-defence** ("by any means necessary") and was initially sceptical of integration\n- He was a powerful orator who articulated the anger and frustration of Black urban communities\n- He criticised the March on Washington as the "farce on Washington"\n\n**Evolution of his views:**\n- After a pilgrimage to Mecca (1964), Malcolm X moved away from the NOI\u2019s racial separatism\n- He began to see racism as a global problem requiring broader alliances\n- He founded the **Organization of Afro-American Unity (OAAU)**\n- He was **assassinated on 21 February 1965** at the Audubon Ballroom in New York\n- His autobiography (published posthumously) became one of the most important books of the era'),
  q(3, 'How did Malcolm X\u2019s views change after his pilgrimage to Mecca in 1964?',
    ['He moved away from racial separatism towards a broader view of racial solidarity', 'He became more radical and advocated armed revolution', 'He joined Martin Luther King\u2019s organisation', 'He left politics entirely'], 0,
    'After his pilgrimage to Mecca, Malcolm X met Muslims of all races and backgrounds. This experience led him to abandon the Nation of Islam\u2019s strict racial separatism and adopt a more inclusive worldview, seeing racism as a global system that required broader alliances to combat.'),
  t(4, '### The Black Panther Party (1966)\n\nThe **Black Panther Party for Self-Defense** was founded in October 1966 in Oakland, California by **Huey P. Newton** and **Bobby Seale**.\n\n**Ten-Point Programme** (key demands):\n1. Freedom and self-determination for the Black community\n2. Full employment\n3. End to exploitation by white business owners\n4. Decent housing\n5. Education that teaches true Black history\n6. Exemption from military service\n7. End to police brutality\n8. Freedom for all Black political prisoners\n9. Fair trials by peers\n10. Land, bread, housing, education, clothing, justice, and peace\n\n**Activities:**\n- Armed patrols monitoring police activity in Black neighbourhoods (legal under California law at the time)\n- **Free Breakfast Program** for school children (feeding thousands of children daily)\n- Free health clinics, legal aid, and education programmes\n- Community empowerment and political education'),
  t(5, '### Stokely Carmichael and "Black Power"\n\n**Stokely Carmichael** (later known as Kwame Ture, 1941\u20131998):\n- Chairman of SNCC from 1966\n- Coined the phrase **"Black Power"** during the March Against Fear in Mississippi (June 1966)\n- Defined Black Power as a call for Black people to unite, recognise their heritage, build a sense of community, define their own goals, and lead their own organisations\n- He rejected the goal of integration, arguing that Black people should build their own institutions\n- Later joined the Black Panther Party as "Honorary Prime Minister"\n- Eventually moved to Guinea, West Africa, and changed his name to **Kwame Ture**\n\n**The concept of Black Power meant different things to different people:**\n- Political power through voting and community organising\n- Economic self-sufficiency and Black-owned businesses\n- Cultural pride: "Black is Beautiful" \u2014 embracing natural hairstyles, African clothing, and African heritage\n- For some, armed self-defence against racist violence'),
  q(6, 'Who coined the phrase "Black Power" during the March Against Fear in 1966?',
    ['Stokely Carmichael', 'Malcolm X', 'Huey Newton', 'Martin Luther King Jr'], 0,
    'Stokely Carmichael, then chairman of SNCC, first used the phrase "Black Power" during the March Against Fear through Mississippi in June 1966. The phrase became a rallying cry for a more militant and self-determined approach to the struggle for Black rights.'),
  t(7, '### Huey P. Newton and the Black Panthers\u2019 Confrontation with the State\n\n**Huey P. Newton** (1942\u20131989):\n- Co-founder and Minister of Defense of the Black Panther Party\n- Intellectual who combined Marxist theory with the practical experience of Black urban poverty\n- Arrested in October 1967 and charged with killing a police officer \u2014 the **"Free Huey"** campaign became a national cause\n\n**Government response:**\n- FBI Director **J. Edgar Hoover** called the Black Panthers *"the greatest threat to the internal security of the country"*\n- The FBI\u2019s **COINTELPRO** programme (Counter Intelligence Program) targeted the Panthers:\n  - Surveillance, infiltration, and disinformation\n  - Fomenting internal divisions\n  - Working with local police to raid Panther offices\n  - The assassination of **Fred Hampton** by Chicago police in December 1969 (while he slept)\n- By the early 1970s, the combined pressure of state repression and internal conflicts had weakened the party'),
  fb(8, 'The FBI programme used to destroy the Black Panther Party was called ___. FBI Director J. Edgar ___ called the Panthers the greatest threat to internal security.',
    ['COINTELPRO', 'Hoover'],
    'COINTELPRO (Counter Intelligence Program) was an FBI programme that used illegal tactics to disrupt and destroy domestic political organisations deemed subversive, including the Black Panthers, SCLC, and other civil rights groups.'),
  t(9, '### Short-term and Long-term Gains\n\n**Short-term gains of the Civil Rights Movement:**\n- **Civil Rights Act (1964)**: Outlawed discrimination based on race, colour, religion, sex, or national origin\n- **Voting Rights Act (1965)**: Eliminated barriers to Black voting in the South\n- Desegregation of schools, buses, restaurants, and public facilities\n- Increased political representation for Black Americans\n\n**Short-term gains of the Black Power Movement:**\n- Increased racial pride and cultural awareness\n- Community programmes (free breakfasts, health clinics)\n- Inspired similar movements globally (including in South Africa)\n\n**Long-term gains:**\n- The election of Black politicians at local, state, and federal levels (culminating in Barack Obama\u2019s election in 2008)\n- Affirmative action policies\n- Greater representation in media, education, and business\n- The legacy of civil rights continues to shape American society\n\n**Limitations:**\n- Economic inequality between Black and white Americans persists\n- Mass incarceration disproportionately affects Black communities\n- Police brutality remains an issue (the Black Lives Matter movement from 2013)\n- De facto segregation continues in housing and education'),
  q(10, 'Which piece of legislation outlawed discrimination based on race, colour, religion, sex, or national origin?',
    ['The Civil Rights Act of 1964', 'The Voting Rights Act of 1965', 'The 13th Amendment', 'The Equal Rights Amendment'], 0,
    'The Civil Rights Act of 1964 was landmark legislation that outlawed discrimination in employment, education, and public accommodations. It was followed by the Voting Rights Act of 1965, which specifically addressed voting rights.'),
  q(11, 'How did the US Civil Rights and Black Power movements connect to the South African struggle against apartheid?',
    ['They inspired similar resistance movements and demonstrated the power of mass mobilisation', 'The ANC sent delegates to join American protests', 'The Black Panthers sent weapons to South Africa', 'There was no connection between the movements'], 0,
    'The US Civil Rights and Black Power movements inspired anti-apartheid activists in South Africa. The Black Consciousness Movement, led by Steve Biko, drew on Black Power ideas of racial pride and self-determination. Protest strategies like boycotts and mass mobilisation were shared across both movements.'),
];

