// --- Lesson 1: Black Consciousness and the 1976 Soweto Uprising ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Civil Resistance in South Africa: Black Consciousness and the 1976 Soweto Uprising\n\n### The Nature and Aims of Black Consciousness\n\nBy the late 1960s, the major anti-apartheid organisations (ANC and PAC) had been banned and their leaders imprisoned or exiled. A new ideology emerged to fill the vacuum: **Black Consciousness (BC)**.\n\n**Key ideas of Black Consciousness:**\n1. **Psychological liberation**: Black people must first free themselves from feelings of inferiority caused by apartheid\n2. **Black pride**: "Black is beautiful" \u2014 rejecting apartheid\u2019s message that Black people were inferior\n3. **Self-reliance**: Black people must lead their own liberation, not depend on white liberals\n4. **Unity**: The term "Black" included all people oppressed by apartheid \u2014 Africans, Coloureds, and Indians\n5. **Community empowerment**: Building Black-run community programmes in education, health, and culture\n\nBlack Consciousness was influenced by:\n- The **Black Power movement** in the USA (Stokely Carmichael, Malcolm X)\n- **Frantz Fanon\u2019s** writings on colonialism and the psychology of the oppressed\n- **African philosophy** and ubuntu\n- **Liberation theology** \u2014 Christianity as a force for social justice'),
  t(2, '### Steve Biko (1946\u20131977)\n\n**Stephen Bantu Biko** was the founder and leading intellectual of the Black Consciousness Movement in South Africa.\n\n**Key milestones:**\n- Born in King William\u2019s Town, Eastern Cape\n- Student at the University of Natal (Medical School)\n- In 1969, helped found the **South African Students\u2019 Organisation (SASO)**, breaking away from the multiracial but white-dominated NUSAS\n- Helped establish the **Black People\u2019s Convention (BPC)** in 1972\n- Banning order in 1973: restricted to the King William\u2019s Town area\n- Despite banning, continued community work through the **Black Community Programmes (BCP)**\n\n**Biko\u2019s philosophy:**\n*"The most potent weapon of the oppressor is the mind of the oppressed."*\n\nHe argued that apartheid\u2019s greatest damage was psychological \u2014 making Black people believe they were inferior. Liberation had to begin in the mind.\n\n**Death in detention:**\n- Arrested on 18 August 1977 under the Terrorism Act\n- Brutally tortured by security police in Port Elizabeth\n- Driven 1,200 km to Pretoria, naked and unconscious, in the back of a Land Rover\n- Died on **12 September 1977** of brain injuries\n- Minister of Police **Jimmy Kruger** infamously said Biko\u2019s death *"leaves me cold"*\n- Biko\u2019s death caused international outrage and intensified sanctions against South Africa'),
  q(3, 'What did Steve Biko mean when he said "The most potent weapon of the oppressor is the mind of the oppressed"?',
    ['That apartheid\u2019s psychological damage \u2014 making Black people feel inferior \u2014 was its most powerful tool of control', 'That the oppressor used mind control technology', 'That education was used to oppress people', 'That Black people needed to use weapons against the oppressor'], 0,
    'Biko argued that the apartheid system\u2019s greatest weapon was not its laws or police, but its ability to make Black people internalise a sense of inferiority. Black Consciousness sought to reverse this psychological damage by instilling pride and self-worth.'),
  t(4, '### The Challenge Posed by BCM to the Apartheid State\n\nThe Black Consciousness Movement challenged the state in several ways:\n\n1. **Ideological challenge**: BCM rejected the apartheid government\u2019s narrative of racial inferiority and separate development (Bantustans)\n2. **Political mobilisation**: Despite operating within the law, BCM organisations politicised a new generation of young Black South Africans\n3. **Rejection of collaboration**: BCM refused to participate in apartheid structures (Bantustan governments, separate councils)\n4. **Community programmes**: Literacy projects, health clinics, and self-help programmes showed that Black communities could organise independently\n5. **Cultural resistance**: Theatre, poetry, and music became vehicles for political consciousness\n\n**Government response:**\n- Banning orders on Biko and other BCM leaders\n- Surveillance and harassment\n- Detention without trial\n- Eventually, the banning of all BCM organisations in **October 1977** (after Biko\u2019s death)'),
  t(5, '### The 1976 Soweto Uprising\n\nThe Soweto Uprising of **16 June 1976** was a watershed moment in South African history.\n\n**Immediate cause:**\n- The apartheid government\u2019s decree that Afrikaans must be used as a medium of instruction in Black schools (alongside English)\n- Students saw Afrikaans as the "language of the oppressor"\n\n**Deeper causes:**\n- Bantu Education was deliberately inferior \u2014 designed to prepare Black children for menial labour\n- Overcrowded classrooms, poorly trained teachers, inadequate resources\n- Rising political consciousness among students, influenced by Black Consciousness\n- General frustration with apartheid oppression\n\n**Events of 16 June 1976:**\n- An estimated **20,000 students** from schools across Soweto marched peacefully to Orlando Stadium\n- Police confronted the students and, without adequate warning, **opened fire**\n- **Hector Pieterson** (aged 12\u201313) was one of the first to be killed \u2014 the photograph of his body being carried became an iconic image of the struggle\n- Violence spread across Soweto and then to townships across South Africa\n- By February 1977, an estimated **575 people** had been killed (mostly by police), thousands injured, and many arrested'),
  q(6, 'What was the immediate cause of the Soweto Uprising of 16 June 1976?',
    ['The government\u2019s decree that Afrikaans must be used as a medium of instruction in Black schools', 'The death of Steve Biko', 'The banning of the ANC', 'An increase in school fees'], 0,
    'The immediate trigger was the Afrikaans language decree, but the uprising reflected broader frustrations with Bantu Education and apartheid. Students rejected Afrikaans as the language of the oppressor.'),
  fb(7, 'The iconic photograph of ___ being carried after being shot during the Soweto Uprising became a symbol of the anti-apartheid struggle. ___ June is now commemorated as Youth Day in South Africa.',
    ['Hector Pieterson', '16'],
    'Hector Pieterson was 12\u201313 years old when he was killed during the Soweto Uprising. The photograph of his body being carried by a fellow student became one of the most powerful images of the anti-apartheid struggle. 16 June is now Youth Day.'),
  t(8, '### Legacy of the Soweto Uprising and Black Consciousness\n\n**Immediate consequences:**\n- International condemnation of apartheid intensified\n- Thousands of young people fled South Africa and joined the ANC and PAC in exile\n- The **UN Security Council** imposed a mandatory arms embargo against South Africa in 1977\n- BCM organisations were banned in October 1977\n- Steve Biko was killed in detention in September 1977\n\n**Long-term legacy:**\n- A new generation of activists was politicised, many of whom would lead the struggle in the 1980s\n- The uprising showed that apartheid could not be sustained indefinitely\n- Black Consciousness ideas continued to influence the struggle even after BCM organisations were banned\n- 16 June became a symbol of youth resistance and is now celebrated as **Youth Day** in South Africa\n- The uprising demonstrated that young people could be powerful agents of change\n\n**Connection to the Civil Rights Movement:**\n- Just as the US Civil Rights Movement inspired Black Consciousness, the Soweto Uprising inspired solidarity movements worldwide\n- Both movements demonstrated that ordinary people, especially young people, could challenge entrenched systems of racial oppression'),
  q(9, 'What was one major consequence of the Soweto Uprising for the anti-apartheid movement?',
    ['Thousands of young people left SA and joined the ANC and PAC in exile', 'The apartheid government immediately began negotiations', 'All political prisoners were released', 'The UN sent military forces to South Africa'], 0,
    'The Soweto Uprising and subsequent repression led to a massive exodus of young South Africans who crossed the borders to join the liberation movements in exile. This gave the ANC and PAC a new generation of trained activists and fighters.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Internal Resistance 1980s ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## The Crisis of the Apartheid State: Internal Resistance in the 1980s\n\n### The Trade Union Movement (from 1973)\n\nThe 1973 **Durban Strikes** were a turning point for the Black labour movement:\n- Over 100,000 workers in Durban went on strike for higher wages\n- The strikes were largely spontaneous and leaderless, making them impossible for the state to suppress by arresting leaders\n- They demonstrated the economic power of Black workers\n\n**Growth of Black trade unions:**\n- The **Wiehahn Commission (1979)** recommended legalising Black trade unions to bring them under state control\n- Instead, unions became a major vehicle for political resistance\n- Key unions: **FOSATU** (Federation of South African Trade Unions) and individual unions like MAWU (Metal and Allied Workers Union)\n- In **1985**, FOSATU and other unions merged to form the **Congress of South African Trade Unions (COSATU)**\n- COSATU aligned with the ANC and played a crucial role in the struggle\n- Unions used **stay-aways** (mass work stoppages) as a political weapon'),
  t(2, '### New Methods of Mobilisation\n\nThe 1980s saw a massive expansion of internal resistance using creative new methods:\n\n**Community organisations:**\n- **Civic associations** (civics) organised residents around local issues: rent, housing, services\n- Township residents boycotted rent payments to Black Local Authorities\n- Consumer boycotts of white-owned businesses put economic pressure on the system\n\n**Religious organisations:**\n- The **South African Council of Churches (SACC)**, led by Archbishop **Desmond Tutu**, was a major voice against apartheid\n- Tutu won the **Nobel Peace Prize** in 1984\n- The **Kairos Document** (1985) challenged churches to take a stand against apartheid\n\n**Student and youth organisations:**\n- **COSAS** (Congress of South African Students) mobilised high school students\n- **NUSAS** and **AZASO** organised university students\n- The slogan *"Liberation before Education"* led to school boycotts, though this was controversial'),
  q(3, 'Which South African religious leader won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1984 for his opposition to apartheid?',
    ['Archbishop Desmond Tutu', 'Steve Biko', 'Nelson Mandela', 'Allan Boesak'], 0,
    'Archbishop Desmond Tutu of the South African Council of Churches won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1984 for his non-violent opposition to apartheid. He used his international platform to call for sanctions and disinvestment.'),
  t(4, '### The United Democratic Front (UDF) \u2014 1983\n\nThe **United Democratic Front (UDF)** was launched on **20 August 1983** in Mitchells Plain, Cape Town.\n\n**Key features:**\n- An umbrella organisation of over **600 affiliated organisations** (civics, youth groups, women\u2019s organisations, religious bodies, trade unions)\n- Broadly aligned with the ANC\u2019s non-racial vision (the Freedom Charter)\n- Led by prominent figures including **Allan Boesak**, **Albertina Sisulu**, and **Archie Gumede**\n\n**Immediate trigger:**\n- Opposition to the **Tricameral Parliament** (1983), which gave Coloureds and Indians limited representation but completely excluded Black Africans\n- The UDF campaigned for a boycott of the Tricameral elections\n\n**Activities:**\n- Coordinated mass campaigns, boycotts, and protests across the country\n- Mobilised millions of people against apartheid\n- Made many townships "ungovernable" through rent boycotts, consumer boycotts, and mass stay-aways\n- The UDF was the most important legal internal resistance organisation of the 1980s'),
  t(5, '### The Mass Democratic Movement (MDM)\n\nAfter the UDF and COSATU were effectively restricted by the state of emergency, the **Mass Democratic Movement (MDM)** emerged in 1989 as an informal coalition:\n- Loose alliance of UDF-aligned organisations, COSATU, and church leaders\n- Organised the **Defiance Campaign** of 1989: mass marches, occupation of whites-only facilities, hospital protests\n- These actions demonstrated that apartheid was becoming unenforceable\n\n### The End Conscription Campaign (ECC)\n\n- Founded in 1983 to oppose **compulsory military service** for white South African men\n- Argued that the SADF was being used to uphold apartheid both inside SA and in neighbouring states (Angola, Namibia)\n- Significant because it was a **white-led** anti-apartheid organisation\n- Demonstrated that opposition to apartheid extended across racial lines\n- Banned in 1988 under the state of emergency\n\n### Black Sash\n\n- Founded in 1955 as the **Women\u2019s Defence of the Constitution League**\n- Mostly white, middle-class women who stood in silent protest wearing black sashes\n- Ran **advice offices** helping Black South Africans navigate apartheid laws (pass laws, removals, pensions)\n- Documented human rights abuses\n- Important because it showed that some white South Africans actively opposed apartheid'),
  fb(6, 'The UDF was launched in 1983 in response to the ___ Parliament, which excluded Black Africans from political representation. The ___ Campaign was a white-led organisation opposing compulsory military service.',
    ['Tricameral', 'End Conscription'],
    'The Tricameral Parliament (1983) created separate chambers for Whites, Coloureds, and Indians but completely excluded Black Africans. The End Conscription Campaign (ECC) opposed the use of white conscripts in defending apartheid.'),
  t(7, '### Government Attempts to Reform Apartheid\n\n**The 1982 Urban Bantu Authorities Act** and related reforms:\n- The apartheid government tried to create a Black middle class with a stake in the system\n- **Black Local Authorities (BLAs)** were given limited powers to govern townships\n- BLAs were widely rejected as puppet bodies \u2014 councillors were seen as collaborators\n- BLAs had to raise their own revenue through rents and service charges, leading to increases that sparked resistance\n\n**The Tricameral System (1983):**\n- Created three separate parliamentary chambers:\n  - **House of Assembly** (White \u2014 maintained overall control)\n  - **House of Representatives** (Coloured)\n  - **House of Delegates** (Indian)\n- Black Africans were completely excluded \u2014 supposed to exercise political rights in the Bantustans\n- Only about **30% of eligible Coloured voters** and **20% of Indian voters** participated \u2014 a massive rejection\n\n**States of Emergency (1985 and 1986):**\n- The government declared states of emergency to suppress the rising tide of resistance\n- Mass detentions, censorship of the media, deployment of troops in townships\n- Over **30,000 people detained** without trial between 1985 and 1990\n- Despite these measures, resistance continued to grow'),
  q(8, 'Why were the Black Local Authorities (BLAs) widely rejected in the townships?',
    ['They were seen as puppet bodies collaborating with the apartheid system', 'They provided excellent services', 'They were elected through free and fair elections', 'They had too much power'], 0,
    'Black Local Authorities were seen as illegitimate bodies created by the apartheid government to give the appearance of self-governance while maintaining white supremacy. BLA councillors were viewed as collaborators and became targets of community anger.'),
  t(9, '### Internal Resistance to Reforms\n\nThe apartheid government\u2019s attempts at reform backfired spectacularly:\n\n1. The Tricameral Parliament **united opposition** rather than dividing it \u2014 leading to the formation of the UDF\n2. BLAs became **targets of community anger** rather than legitimate governing bodies\n3. The Vaal Triangle Uprising (September 1984) \u2014 protests against rent increases by BLAs led to widespread violence and the beginning of the "township revolt"\n4. The slogan **"Asinamali!"** ("We have no money!") reflected the economic dimension of resistance\n5. By the late 1980s, the apartheid system was in deep crisis:\n   - Economic sanctions and disinvestment were devastating the economy\n   - International isolation was complete\n   - The war in Angola and Namibia was costly\n   - Internal resistance made the country ungovernable\n   - The ideological justification for apartheid had collapsed\n\nThis crisis set the stage for the negotiations that would lead to democracy in 1994.'),
  q(10, 'What was the significance of the Vaal Triangle Uprising of September 1984?',
    ['It marked the beginning of sustained township revolt that would continue through the 1980s', 'It led to the immediate end of apartheid', 'It was the first protest since the Soweto Uprising', 'It resulted in the release of Nelson Mandela'], 0,
    'The Vaal Triangle Uprising, triggered by rent increases imposed by Black Local Authorities, marked the beginning of a sustained period of township revolt. It showed that the government\u2019s reform strategy had failed and that resistance was intensifying.'),
];

