// --- Lesson 1: The Truth and Reconciliation Commission (TRC) ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## The Truth and Reconciliation Commission (TRC)\n\n### Reasons for the TRC Hearings\n\nAfter South Africa\u2019s transition to democracy in 1994, the new government faced a profound question: how to deal with the human rights violations committed during the apartheid era?\n\n**Options considered:**\n1. **Nuremberg-style trials**: Prosecute all perpetrators (as done with Nazi war criminals)\n   - Risk: Could destabilise the fragile new democracy; former security forces might resist\n2. **General amnesty**: Forgive everything and move on\n   - Risk: Victims would receive no justice or acknowledgement\n3. **Truth commission**: A middle path \u2014 uncover the truth in exchange for amnesty\n   - This was the path chosen\n\nThe **Promotion of National Unity and Reconciliation Act (1995)** established the TRC.\n\n**Purpose:**\n- To establish a complete picture of the nature, causes, and extent of gross human rights violations committed between **1 March 1960 and 10 May 1994**\n- To grant amnesty to individuals who made full disclosure of politically motivated acts\n- To recommend reparations for victims\n- To promote national unity and reconciliation'),
  t(2, '### Structure of the TRC\n\nThe TRC was chaired by Archbishop **Desmond Tutu** and had three committees:\n\n| Committee | Function |\n|-----------|----------|\n| **Human Rights Violations Committee** | Heard testimony from victims of gross human rights violations |\n| **Amnesty Committee** | Considered applications for amnesty from perpetrators |\n| **Reparation and Rehabilitation Committee** | Recommended reparations for victims |\n\n**Key principles:**\n- **Public hearings**: Victims could tell their stories publicly, restoring their dignity\n- **Amnesty in exchange for truth**: Perpetrators could receive amnesty IF they made **full disclosure** of **politically motivated** acts\n- **Restorative justice** rather than retributive justice: focused on healing rather than punishment\n\nThe TRC heard testimony from over **21,000 victims** and received over **7,000 amnesty applications**. Hearings were broadcast on television and radio, ensuring the nation confronted its past.'),
  q(3, 'What condition did perpetrators have to meet to receive amnesty from the TRC?',
    ['Full disclosure of politically motivated acts', 'Payment of compensation to victims', 'A public apology', 'A prison sentence of at least five years'], 0,
    'To receive amnesty, perpetrators had to make full disclosure of all relevant facts and demonstrate that their actions were politically motivated. If they withheld information or if their acts were motivated by personal gain or malice, amnesty was denied.'),
  t(4, '### Debates Concerning the TRC\n\nThe TRC was highly controversial and generated intense debate:\n\n**Arguments in favour:**\n- It uncovered truths that might otherwise have remained hidden\n- It gave victims a voice and restored their dignity\n- It prevented a cycle of vengeance and retribution\n- It promoted national healing and reconciliation\n- It created a shared historical record\n\n**Arguments against:**\n- Amnesty meant perpetrators could escape punishment\n- Many victims felt that truth without justice was inadequate\n- Some argued it created a **moral equivalence** between apartheid forces and liberation movements\n- The apartheid government systematically destroyed documents before 1994, limiting what could be uncovered\n- Many senior political and military leaders refused to apply for amnesty or cooperate\n- The TRC could not compel the business community or judiciary to account for their roles in apartheid'),
  t(5, '### Case Studies of Victims and Perpetrators\n\n**Victims:**\n- The **Cradock Four**: Matthew Goniwe, Fort Calata, Sparrow Mkhonto, and Sicelo Mhlauli \u2014 anti-apartheid activists murdered by security police in 1985. The TRC revealed the role of the State Security Council.\n- **Steve Biko**: The TRC heard testimony about his torture and death in detention, but his killers\u2019 amnesty application was **denied** because they lied about the circumstances.\n- The families of the **Gugulethu Seven** \u2014 young men lured by a police informer and then killed by police in 1986.\n\n**Perpetrators:**\n- **Eugene de Kock** ("Prime Evil"): Commander of the Vlakplaas death squad. Convicted of six murders and other crimes. Applied for amnesty for many acts. Some applications were granted, others denied.\n- **Jeffrey Benzien**: A security police officer who demonstrated his torture method (the "wet bag" technique) at a TRC hearing \u2014 one of the most disturbing moments of the commission.\n- **Winnie Madikizela-Mandela**: The TRC found her responsible for gross human rights violations in connection with the Mandela United Football Club, including the murder of **Stompie Seipei** (a 14-year-old activist).'),
  q(6, 'Why was the amnesty application of Steve Biko\u2019s killers denied by the TRC?',
    ['They did not make full disclosure and lied about the circumstances of his death', 'Biko\u2019s family refused to participate', 'Their acts were not politically motivated', 'The time limit for applications had passed'], 0,
    'The TRC denied amnesty to the officers responsible for Biko\u2019s death because they failed to make full disclosure. They lied about the circumstances of his torture and death, which was the fundamental requirement for amnesty.'),
  fb(7, 'The TRC was chaired by Archbishop Desmond ___. It focused on ___ justice (healing) rather than retributive justice (punishment).',
    ['Tutu', 'restorative'],
    'Archbishop Desmond Tutu chaired the TRC. The concept of restorative justice seeks to repair the harm done and restore relationships, rather than focusing solely on punishing offenders.'),
  t(8, '### Amnesty, Human Rights, and Reparations\n\n**Amnesty outcomes:**\n- Of approximately 7,112 amnesty applications, only about **1,500 were granted**\n- Many applications were denied for lack of full disclosure or because acts were not politically motivated\n- Some prominent figures (including former President P.W. Botha) refused to cooperate and were prosecuted\n\n**Reparations:**\n- The TRC recommended that each victim receive **R21,700 per year for six years** (approximately R130,000 total)\n- The government eventually made a **one-off payment of R30,000** per victim in 2003 \u2014 far less than recommended\n- Many victims and families felt the reparations were inadequate\n- The failure to implement full reparations remains one of the TRC\u2019s greatest criticisms\n\n**Responses of political parties:**\n- **ANC**: Supported the TRC but was uncomfortable with findings about violations committed by its own members in exile camps\n- **National Party (NP)**: Former President F.W. de Klerk initially cooperated but later tried to suppress parts of the report\n- **Inkatha Freedom Party (IFP)**: Chief Mangosuthu Buthelezi refused to appear before the TRC\n- The TRC\u2019s final report was critical of **all parties** who committed human rights violations'),
  q(9, 'How did the government\u2019s actual reparation payments compare to the TRC\u2019s recommendation?',
    ['The government paid R30,000 per victim, far less than the recommended R130,000', 'The government paid more than recommended', 'The government paid exactly what was recommended', 'No reparations were ever paid'], 0,
    'The TRC recommended approximately R130,000 per victim (R21,700 per year for six years). The government instead made a one-off payment of R30,000 per victim in 2003. This significant shortfall remains a major source of criticism.'),
  t(10, '### The TRC\u2019s Final Report and Legacy\n\nThe TRC submitted its final report in **October 1998** (with additional volumes in 2003).\n\n**Key findings:**\n- The apartheid state was responsible for the **majority** of gross human rights violations\n- The ANC also committed human rights violations, particularly in its exile camps\n- The IFP was responsible for widespread political violence, often in collaboration with security forces (the "Third Force")\n- Business, the judiciary, the media, and the health profession were complicit in maintaining apartheid\n\n**Legacy:**\n- The TRC created a shared public record of apartheid\u2019s horrors\n- It demonstrated that transitional justice is possible (the TRC model has been adopted by other countries)\n- However, reconciliation remains incomplete \u2014 many victims feel that justice was not served\n- Economic inequality, largely along racial lines, continues to divide South African society\n- The TRC\u2019s work raises important questions: Can there be reconciliation without justice? Is truth enough?'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: The Negotiated Settlement ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## The Negotiated Settlement: The Road to Democracy\n\n### Beginning of Negotiations (1990\u20131991)\n\nBy the late 1980s, it was clear that neither the apartheid government nor the liberation movements could achieve an outright victory. Several factors pushed both sides towards negotiation:\n\n**Factors favouring negotiation:**\n1. **Economic crisis**: Sanctions, disinvestment, and the cost of maintaining apartheid were devastating the economy\n2. **Military stalemate**: The Border War (Angola/Namibia) had ended in a draw\n3. **End of the Cold War**: The collapse of the Soviet Union removed the communist threat that the government had used to justify apartheid\n4. **Internal resistance**: The country was becoming ungovernable\n5. **International pressure**: South Africa was completely isolated diplomatically\n6. **Secret talks**: Mandela had been in secret discussions with the government since 1986\n\n**2 February 1990: F.W. de Klerk\u2019s historic speech:**\n- Unbanned the ANC, PAC, SACP, and other organisations\n- Announced the imminent release of Nelson Mandela\n- Lifted the state of emergency\n- Committed to negotiations for a new constitution'),
  t(2, '### Nelson Mandela\u2019s Release (11 February 1990)\n\n**Nelson Rolihlahla Mandela** walked out of Victor Verster Prison on **11 February 1990**, after 27 years of imprisonment.\n\n- His release was broadcast live to a global audience of hundreds of millions\n- In his speech, he reaffirmed his commitment to a negotiated settlement and a non-racial democracy\n- He called on all South Africans to work together for peace\n\n**Obstacles to negotiations:**\n- Deep mutual distrust between the government and liberation movements\n- Political violence, particularly between ANC (Xhosa-aligned) and IFP (Zulu-aligned) supporters in KwaZulu-Natal and the Witwatersrand\n- Evidence of a "**Third Force**" \u2014 elements within the security forces fomenting violence to derail negotiations\n- The **Bisho massacre** (September 1992) \u2014 Ciskei homeland soldiers killed 28 ANC supporters\n- Differences on the constitutional framework: the NP wanted power-sharing guarantees; the ANC wanted majority rule'),
  q(3, 'On what date was Nelson Mandela released from prison?',
    ['11 February 1990', '2 February 1990', '27 April 1994', '16 June 1976'], 0,
    'Nelson Mandela was released from Victor Verster Prison on 11 February 1990, after 27 years of imprisonment. His release followed F.W. de Klerk\u2019s historic speech on 2 February 1990 unbanning the ANC and other organisations.'),
  t(4, '### Breakdown of Negotiations\n\nNegotiations did not proceed smoothly:\n\n**CODESA I (December 1991):**\n- The **Convention for a Democratic South Africa** brought together 19 political parties and organisations\n- Made progress on principles for a new constitution\n- Disagreement over the meaning of "sufficient consensus"\n\n**CODESA II (May 1992):**\n- Collapsed over disputes about:\n  - How much power the central government vs regions would have\n  - Whether there should be a two-thirds or 75% majority requirement for constitutional decisions\n  - Transitional arrangements\n\n**The Boipatong Massacre (June 1992):**\n- IFP-aligned hostel dwellers attacked the township of Boipatong, killing 45 people\n- Evidence emerged that police had assisted the attackers\n- Mandela withdrew the ANC from negotiations, accusing the government of complicity in violence\n- This was the lowest point in the negotiation process'),
  t(5, '### Multi-Party Process Resumes\n\n**The Record of Understanding (September 1992):**\n- After the Bisho massacre (September 1992), both the ANC and the government realised that continued violence could lead to catastrophe\n- Mandela and De Klerk signed the **Record of Understanding**, committing to:\n  - Release of political prisoners\n  - Fencing of hostels (to reduce violence)\n  - Banning of dangerous weapons at public gatherings\n- This bilateral agreement sidelined more extreme parties on both sides\n\n**The Multi-Party Negotiating Process (MPNP) \u2014 1993:**\n- Resumed at the **World Trade Centre** in Kempton Park\n- 26 parties participated\n- Produced an **Interim Constitution** that would govern the transition\n- Key compromises:\n  - A **Government of National Unity (GNU)** for five years\n  - Any party with more than 5% of the vote would be included in the cabinet\n  - A **Constitutional Court** to protect fundamental rights\n  - Provincial governments with some powers'),
  fb(6, 'The first multiparty negotiations were called ___ (Convention for a Democratic South Africa). The negotiations resumed at the World Trade Centre in ___ Park.',
    ['CODESA', 'Kempton'],
    'CODESA (Convention for a Democratic South Africa) was the first formal multiparty negotiation forum. When talks resumed in 1993, they were held at the World Trade Centre in Kempton Park as the Multi-Party Negotiating Process.'),
  t(7, '### Ongoing Violence and the Assassination of Chris Hani\n\nViolence continued to threaten the peace process:\n\n**The assassination of Chris Hani (10 April 1993):**\n- Chris Hani, leader of the SACP and former MK chief of staff, was assassinated outside his home in Boksburg\n- He was shot by **Janusz Walu\u015b**, a Polish immigrant, acting with **Clive Derby-Lewis** (Conservative Party MP)\n- Hani was extremely popular, especially among young Black South Africans\n- His death could have sparked a revolution, but **Mandela went on national television** and called for calm\n- This moment demonstrated Mandela\u2019s extraordinary leadership and the ANC\u2019s commitment to a negotiated solution\n- It also accelerated the setting of an **election date**: 27 April 1994\n\n**Right-wing resistance:**\n- The Conservative Party and Afrikaner Weerstandsbeweging (AWB) rejected negotiations\n- In June 1993, AWB members drove an armoured vehicle into the World Trade Centre\n- Right-wing violence in Bophuthatswana (March 1994) was a turning point: when AWB members were shot and killed, the far right\u2019s armed resistance collapsed\n- The Freedom Front (under General Constand Viljoen) chose to participate in elections'),
  q(8, 'How did Nelson Mandela respond to the assassination of Chris Hani in April 1993?',
    ['He went on national television to call for calm and recommit to the negotiation process', 'He called for armed resistance', 'He suspended all negotiations', 'He fled the country'], 0,
    'Mandela\u2019s televised appeal for calm after Hani\u2019s assassination is considered one of his most important moments of leadership. He effectively stepped into the role of national leader, demonstrating that he could unite the country in a moment of crisis.'),
  t(9, '### The Final Road to Democracy\n\n**27 April 1994 \u2014 South Africa\u2019s first democratic election:**\n- Millions of South Africans of all races voted in long, patient queues\n- It was the first election in which **all South Africans** could vote regardless of race\n- Many people waited for hours, some for an entire day\n- The election was largely peaceful\n\n**Results:**\n\n| Party | Percentage | Seats |\n|-------|-----------|-------|\n| ANC | 62.6% | 252 |\n| NP | 20.4% | 82 |\n| IFP | 10.5% | 43 |\n| Freedom Front | 2.2% | 9 |\n| DP | 1.7% | 7 |\n| PAC | 1.2% | 5 |\n\n**The Government of National Unity:**\n- **Nelson Mandela** became the first democratically elected President of South Africa\n- **Thabo Mbeki** (ANC) and **F.W. de Klerk** (NP) became Deputy Presidents\n- IFP was included in the cabinet (Buthelezi became Minister of Home Affairs)\n- The GNU represented a genuine attempt at inclusive governance\n\nThe 1994 election was hailed as a "**miracle**" \u2014 a peaceful transition from racial oppression to democracy, achieved through negotiation rather than civil war.'),
  fb(10, 'South Africa\u2019s first democratic election took place on ___ April 1994. The ANC won with ___% of the vote.',
    ['27', '62.6'],
    'The election of 27 April 1994 was the first time all South Africans could vote regardless of race. The ANC won 62.6% of the vote, and Nelson Mandela became president.'),
  q(11, 'Why is South Africa\u2019s transition to democracy often described as a "miracle"?',
    ['Because it was achieved through negotiation and compromise despite enormous potential for civil war', 'Because it happened overnight', 'Because no violence occurred during the apartheid era', 'Because foreign countries imposed democracy'], 0,
    'Given the deep divisions, decades of oppression, and ongoing violence, many observers expected South Africa\u2019s transition to end in civil war. The fact that it was achieved through negotiation, compromise, and a largely peaceful election earned it the description of a "miracle."'),
];

