// --- Lesson 1: Gorbachev\u2019s Reforms and Eastern Europe ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch6_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## The End of the Cold War and the New World Order\n\n### Gorbachev\u2019s Reforms in the Soviet Union\n\nBy the 1980s, the Soviet Union was in deep crisis:\n- **Economic stagnation**: Central planning was inefficient; consumer goods were scarce; technology lagged behind the West\n- **Military overspending**: The arms race and the war in Afghanistan (1979\u20131989) drained resources\n- **Political rigidity**: An aging, unresponsive leadership (Brezhnev, Andropov, Chernenko all died in quick succession)\n- **Social problems**: Alcoholism, corruption, environmental disasters (notably Chernobyl in 1986)\n\n**Mikhail Gorbachev** became General Secretary of the Communist Party in **March 1985**. He was 54, relatively young, and recognised that fundamental reform was necessary for the Soviet system to survive.'),
  t(2, '### Glasnost and Perestroika\n\n**Glasnost ("openness"):**\n- Greater freedom of speech and press\n- Open criticism of government policies and officials was permitted\n- Previously banned books and films were released\n- The full extent of Stalinist crimes was acknowledged\n- However, glasnost also unleashed nationalist movements in Soviet republics\n\n**Perestroika ("restructuring"):**\n- Economic reforms introducing elements of market economics\n- Allowed limited private enterprise and cooperatives\n- Reduced central planning\n- However, the reforms were half-hearted and inconsistent\n- The economy actually worsened during the transition period\n- Shortages of basic goods increased, leading to public frustration\n\n**"New Thinking" in foreign policy:**\n- Gorbachev reduced Soviet commitments abroad\n- Withdrew Soviet troops from Afghanistan (1989)\n- Signed arms reduction treaties with the USA (INF Treaty, 1987)\n- Declared that the Soviet Union would no longer use force to maintain communist governments in Eastern Europe \u2014 the **"Sinatra Doctrine"** ("they can do it their way")'),
  q(3, 'What was the significance of Gorbachev\u2019s "Sinatra Doctrine"?',
    ['The USSR would no longer use force to maintain communist governments in Eastern Europe', 'The USSR would adopt American cultural values', 'The USSR would increase military spending', 'The USSR would annex Eastern European countries'], 0,
    'The "Sinatra Doctrine" (named after Frank Sinatra\u2019s song "My Way") replaced the Brezhnev Doctrine. It meant the Soviet Union would allow Eastern European countries to determine their own political systems, effectively removing the threat of Soviet military intervention.'),
  t(4, '### Events in Eastern Europe\n\n**Poland \u2014 The Role of Solidarity:**\n- **Solidarity** (Solidarno\u015b\u0107) was a trade union and social movement founded in 1980 at the Gdansk shipyard, led by **Lech Walesa**\n- It had over 10 million members \u2014 the first independent trade union in a Soviet-bloc country\n- Martial law was declared in 1981 and Solidarity was banned\n- But the movement survived underground\n- In **1989**, the government was forced to negotiate with Solidarity\n- **Round Table Agreements** (April 1989): partially free elections were held\n- Solidarity won 99 of 100 Senate seats and all contestable Sejm (lower house) seats\n- In August 1989, **Tadeusz Mazowiecki** became the first non-communist prime minister in Eastern Europe since the 1940s\n- This inspired similar movements across Eastern Europe\n\n**The significance of Poland:**\n- Poland showed that peaceful, mass-based civic movements could defeat communist regimes\n- Solidarity\u2019s success emboldened opposition movements across Eastern Europe\n- The Soviet Union\u2019s decision not to intervene confirmed the "Sinatra Doctrine"'),
  fb(5, 'The Polish trade union movement ___ was led by Lech Walesa. In 1989, Poland became the first Eastern European country to elect a non-communist ___ since the 1940s.',
    ['Solidarity', 'prime minister'],
    'Solidarity (Solidarno\u015b\u0107) was a mass movement of over 10 million members. Tadeusz Mazowiecki became the first non-communist prime minister in Eastern Europe since the 1940s, following the Round Table Agreements of 1989.'),
  t(6, '### The Fall of the Berlin Wall (9 November 1989)\n\nThe Berlin Wall, built in 1961, had been the most powerful symbol of the Cold War division of Europe.\n\n**Events leading to the fall:**\n- Hungary opened its border with Austria in May 1989, allowing thousands of East Germans to flee to the West through Hungary\n- Mass demonstrations in East German cities, especially the **Monday Demonstrations** in Leipzig\n- Hundreds of thousands demanded reform: *"Wir sind das Volk!"* ("We are the people!")\n- On **9 November 1989**, an East German government spokesman announced that the borders were open, effective immediately\n- Thousands of East and West Berliners rushed to the Wall, celebrating and tearing it apart\n\n**Significance:**\n- The fall of the Wall was the most dramatic moment of the end of the Cold War\n- It symbolised the end of the division of Europe\n- German reunification followed on **3 October 1990**\n- The fall of the Wall triggered a domino effect across Eastern Europe:\n  - Czechoslovakia: the **Velvet Revolution** (November 1989)\n  - Romania: violent overthrow of Ceau\u0219escu (December 1989)\n  - Bulgaria, Hungary, and others all transitioned away from communism'),
  q(7, 'What was the immediate event that triggered the opening of the Berlin Wall on 9 November 1989?',
    ['An East German government spokesman announced that the borders were open', 'NATO forces broke through the Wall', 'Gorbachev ordered the Wall to be demolished', 'A revolution in East Germany overthrew the government'], 0,
    'On the evening of 9 November 1989, East German government spokesman G\u00fcnter Schabowski announced new travel regulations, mistakenly saying they were effective immediately. Thousands of East Berliners rushed to the border crossings, and overwhelmed guards eventually opened the gates.'),
  t(8, '### The Disintegration of the Soviet Union\n\nGorbachev\u2019s reforms, intended to save the Soviet system, ultimately destroyed it:\n\n**Key events:**\n- Glasnost unleashed **nationalist movements** in Soviet republics (Baltic states, Ukraine, Georgia, etc.)\n- The Baltic states (Lithuania, Latvia, Estonia) declared independence in 1990\n- **Hardline coup attempt** (August 1991): Communist hardliners tried to overthrow Gorbachev\n- **Boris Yeltsin**, President of the Russian Republic, stood on a tank outside the Russian parliament and rallied opposition to the coup\n- The coup failed, but it fatally weakened Gorbachev\u2019s authority\n- Republic after republic declared independence\n- On **25 December 1991**, Gorbachev resigned as president of the Soviet Union\n- On **26 December 1991**, the Soviet Union was officially dissolved\n- It was replaced by the **Commonwealth of Independent States (CIS)**\n\nThe dissolution of the Soviet Union ended the Cold War and the bipolar world order that had defined international politics since 1945.'),
  fb(9, 'The Soviet Union was officially dissolved on 26 December ___. Boris ___ played a crucial role in resisting the August 1991 hardline coup attempt.',
    ['1991', 'Yeltsin'],
    'The Soviet Union ceased to exist on 26 December 1991. Boris Yeltsin\u2019s dramatic stand against the August 1991 coup attempt made him a hero of Russian democracy, though his subsequent presidency would be controversial.'),
  t(10, '### Impact on South Africa\n\nThe end of the Cold War had a profound impact on South Africa:\n\n1. **Removed the "communist threat" justification**: The apartheid government could no longer claim it was defending the West against communism\n2. **Changed Western attitudes**: Western governments that had tolerated apartheid as a Cold War ally now increased pressure for reform\n3. **Affected the ANC**: The loss of Soviet support meant the ANC had to reconsider its strategies and alliances\n4. **Facilitated negotiations**: With the Cold War over, both sides lost their main external backers, making negotiation more attractive\n5. **Influenced economic policy**: The collapse of communism pushed the ANC towards a more market-oriented economic approach\n\n**The global turning point:**\n- F.W. de Klerk later cited the fall of the Berlin Wall as a factor in his decision to unban the ANC\n- The end of the Cold War created a window of opportunity for peaceful change in South Africa\n- Mandela\u2019s release (February 1990) came just three months after the fall of the Berlin Wall (November 1989)'),
  q(11, 'How did the end of the Cold War contribute to the end of apartheid in South Africa?',
    ['It removed the government\u2019s justification of fighting communism and increased Western pressure for reform', 'Western countries invaded South Africa', 'The ANC defeated the SADF militarily', 'The United Nations imposed military sanctions'], 0,
    'The end of the Cold War undermined the apartheid government\u2019s claim that it was a Western ally against communism. Western nations that had tolerated apartheid could no longer justify their support, increasing pressure on the South African government to negotiate.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: The New World Order and Globalisation ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch6_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## The New World Order and Globalisation\n\n### A New Global World Order\n\nThe end of the Cold War created a fundamentally different international system:\n\n**From bipolar to unipolar (and then multipolar):**\n- The Cold War world was **bipolar**: dominated by two superpowers (USA and USSR)\n- After 1991, the USA emerged as the sole superpower (**unipolar** world)\n- Over time, new powers emerged (China, EU, India, Brazil), creating a **multipolar** world\n\n**US President George H.W. Bush** declared a "New World Order" in 1991:\n- International cooperation through the United Nations\n- Promotion of democracy and free markets\n- The rule of law in international relations\n\n**However, the new world order also brought new challenges:**\n- Ethnic conflicts (Yugoslavia, Rwanda)\n- The rise of terrorism (culminating in the 11 September 2001 attacks)\n- Nuclear proliferation concerns\n- Economic inequality between developed and developing nations\n- Environmental challenges (climate change)'),
  t(2, '### Globalisation\n\n**What is globalisation?**\nGlobalisation is the increasing interconnectedness of the world\u2019s economies, cultures, and populations, driven by trade, investment, technology, and the movement of people.\n\n**Key features of globalisation:**\n\n| Aspect | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Economic** | Free trade, multinational corporations, global supply chains, World Trade Organization (WTO) |\n| **Cultural** | Spread of Western (especially American) culture \u2014 music, films, fast food, social media |\n| **Technological** | Internet, mobile phones, social media connecting people worldwide |\n| **Political** | International organisations (UN, AU, EU), human rights norms |\n| **Environmental** | Global challenges (climate change, pollution) requiring international cooperation |\n\n**Drivers of globalisation:**\n- End of the Cold War opened up previously closed economies\n- Advances in transport and communication technology\n- Neoliberal economic policies (privatisation, deregulation, free trade)\n- The rise of the Internet (from the 1990s)'),
  q(3, 'What type of world order emerged immediately after the collapse of the Soviet Union?',
    ['Unipolar, with the USA as the sole superpower', 'Bipolar, with the USA and China', 'Multipolar, with many equal powers', 'Anarchic, with no dominant power'], 0,
    'After the collapse of the Soviet Union in 1991, the USA emerged as the world\u2019s sole superpower, creating a unipolar international system. Over time, the rise of China, the EU, and other powers has moved the world towards a multipolar system.'),
  t(4, '### Arguments For and Against Globalisation\n\n**Arguments in favour:**\n- Economic growth and poverty reduction (especially in China and India)\n- Access to goods, services, and information from around the world\n- Cultural exchange and understanding\n- Spread of democratic values and human rights norms\n- Medical and scientific advances shared globally\n\n**Arguments against:**\n- Increased inequality: wealth concentrates in developed countries and among elites\n- Exploitation of workers in developing countries (sweatshops, low wages)\n- Environmental destruction: global trade increases pollution and resource depletion\n- Cultural homogenisation: local cultures and languages are threatened by Western dominance\n- Loss of national sovereignty: governments constrained by international trade rules and multinational corporations\n- The benefits of globalisation are not equally distributed'),
  t(5, '### Globalisation and South Africa\n\nSouth Africa\u2019s experience of globalisation has been complex:\n\n**Positive effects:**\n- Re-integration into the global economy after decades of sanctions and isolation\n- Foreign investment and trade opportunities\n- Access to global markets for South African products (wine, fruit, minerals)\n- Technology transfer and access to the Internet\n- Cultural exchange and the rise of South African music, film, and literature on the world stage\n\n**Negative effects:**\n- Competition from cheap imports has devastated local manufacturing (especially textiles and clothing)\n- Job losses in industries unable to compete globally\n- Brain drain: skilled South Africans emigrating to developed countries\n- Growing inequality: the benefits of globalisation have largely accrued to the already wealthy\n- Environmental costs of mining and resource extraction for global markets\n\n**The debate continues:**\n- Is globalisation an opportunity for development, or a new form of economic colonialism?\n- How can developing countries benefit from globalisation while protecting their workers and environments?\n- What is the role of international organisations in ensuring fair globalisation?'),
  fb(6, 'The increasing interconnectedness of the world\u2019s economies, cultures, and populations is called ___. After apartheid, South Africa was able to re-integrate into the global ___ after decades of isolation.',
    ['globalisation', 'economy'],
    'Globalisation describes the growing interconnectedness of the world. South Africa\u2019s transition to democracy in 1994 allowed it to rejoin the global economy after years of sanctions and diplomatic isolation.'),
  q(7, 'Which of the following is a negative effect of globalisation on South Africa?',
    ['Competition from cheap imports devastating local manufacturing', 'Increased access to global markets', 'Re-integration into the global economy', 'Access to new technology'], 0,
    'While globalisation brought many benefits to South Africa, cheap imports \u2014 particularly in textiles and clothing \u2014 have led to factory closures and job losses. This is one of the key negative effects of globalisation on developing economies.'),
  t(8, '### Source Analysis: Interpreting Different Perspectives on the Cold War and Globalisation\n\n**When analysing sources on these topics, consider:**\n\n**On the Cold War:**\n- Western sources tend to emphasise Soviet aggression and the value of containment\n- Soviet/Russian sources emphasise Western provocation and the threat of US imperialism\n- Third World/developing country sources emphasise the devastating impact of superpower rivalry on their countries\n\n**On globalisation:**\n- Pro-globalisation sources (often from developed countries or international organisations) emphasise economic growth and opportunity\n- Anti-globalisation sources (often from developing countries, labour movements, or environmentalists) emphasise inequality and exploitation\n\n**Key source analysis questions:**\n1. Who created this source and what is their perspective?\n2. What is the purpose of the source?\n3. How reliable is the source? Does it have first-hand knowledge?\n4. How useful is the source for understanding the topic?\n5. What are the limitations of the source?\n6. How does this source compare with other sources on the same topic?'),
  q(9, 'When analysing a source about the Cold War written by a US government official in 1950, what should you consider?',
    ['The source likely reflects a Western/anti-Soviet bias and the official\u2019s role in the government', 'The source is automatically reliable because it comes from a government official', 'The source is useless because it is biased', 'The source represents an objective, neutral perspective'], 0,
    'A source from a US government official in 1950 would likely reflect the Western perspective and Cold War biases of that era. However, it is still useful: it reveals American attitudes and thinking, even if it presents a one-sided view. No source is completely objective.'),
];

