blockNum = 0;
const ch7_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Revision and Exam Preparation: Grade 12 History\n\n### Exam Structure\n\n**Paper 1** (150 marks, 3 hours):\n- Cold War origins and extension\n- Independent Africa (Angola and/or Congo)\n- Civil society protests (US Civil Rights Movement and/or Black Power Movement)\n\n**Paper 2** (150 marks, 3 hours):\n- Civil resistance in South Africa 1970s\u20131980s (Black Consciousness, internal resistance)\n- The Truth and Reconciliation Commission (TRC)\n- The End of the Cold War and the New World Order\n\n**Question types:**\n1. **Source-based questions** (approximately 75 marks per paper)\n2. **Essay questions** (approximately 75 marks per paper, usually two essays of 50 marks each or a combination)'),
  t(2, '### Source-Based Questions Technique\n\nSource-based questions test your ability to analyse, interpret, and evaluate historical sources.\n\n**Types of source-based questions and how to answer them:**\n\n| Question type | What to do |\n|--------------|------------|\n| **Identify/Name** | Give a short, direct answer from the source |\n| **Explain** | Give a detailed answer using the source AND your own knowledge |\n| **Quote/Extract** | Copy the relevant words directly from the source |\n| **Compare** | Identify similarities and/or differences between sources |\n| **Evaluate reliability** | Consider who created it, when, why, and whether it has first-hand knowledge |\n| **Evaluate usefulness** | Does the source help answer the question? What information does it provide? What are its limitations? |\n| **Bias** | Identify the creator\u2019s perspective. Does the source present one side only? What is the purpose? |\n| **Define/Explain a concept** | Use your own knowledge to explain the term in context |'),
  q(3, 'When evaluating the reliability of a historical source, which of the following is MOST important to consider?',
    ['Who created the source, when, and for what purpose', 'Whether the source is long or short', 'Whether the source is printed or handwritten', 'The language the source is written in'], 0,
    'Reliability depends on the author\u2019s knowledge, perspective, purpose, and the context in which the source was created. A source\u2019s length or format does not determine its reliability.'),
  t(4, '### Essay Writing Technique: The PEEL Method\n\nHistory essays require you to construct an **argument** supported by evidence.\n\n**The PEEL structure for each paragraph:**\n\n| Letter | Meaning | Explanation |\n|--------|---------|-------------|\n| **P** | Point | Make a clear point that relates to the question |\n| **E** | Evidence | Support your point with specific historical evidence (dates, names, events) |\n| **E** | Explain | Explain how the evidence supports your point |\n| **L** | Link | Link back to the question / thesis statement |\n\n**Essay structure (50 marks):**\n\n1. **Introduction** (approximately 5 marks):\n   - Define key terms\n   - Provide context\n   - State your thesis (your argument/line of argument)\n\n2. **Body** (approximately 35\u201340 marks):\n   - 3\u20135 PEEL paragraphs\n   - Each paragraph makes ONE main point\n   - Use specific evidence: dates, names, events, statistics\n   - Show different perspectives where relevant\n\n3. **Conclusion** (approximately 5 marks):\n   - Summarise your argument\n   - Give a final judgement\n   - DO NOT introduce new information'),
  fb(5, 'The essay writing method PEEL stands for Point, ___, Explain, and Link. A good history essay should include specific ___ such as dates, names, and events.',
    ['Evidence', 'evidence'],
    'PEEL helps structure persuasive paragraphs: make a Point, support it with Evidence, Explain the significance, and Link back to the question. Specific evidence (dates, names, events, statistics) strengthens your argument.'),
  t(6, '### Key Content Review: Paper 1 Topics\n\n**Cold War Origins:**\n- Yalta and Potsdam conferences; ideological differences; Truman Doctrine; Marshall Plan; Berlin Blockade and Airlift; NATO and Warsaw Pact; Iron Curtain\n\n**Vietnam:**\n- French Indochina; Geneva Accords; Domino Theory; Gulf of Tonkin; Tet Offensive; Vietnamisation; Fall of Saigon; impact on US domestic politics\n\n**Angola:**\n- Portuguese colonialism; Carnation Revolution; MPLA, FNLA, UNITA; role of USSR, Cuba, USA, SA; Battle of Cuito Cuanavale; New York Accords\n\n**Congo:**\n- Belgian colonialism; Lumumba; Congo Crisis; Katanga secession; CIA involvement; Mobutu; resource curse\n\n**US Civil Rights:**\n- Jim Crow laws; Brown v Board of Education; Montgomery Bus Boycott; sit-ins; Birmingham; March on Washington; Selma; Little Rock; MLK\n\n**Black Power:**\n- Malcolm X; Black Panther Party; Stokely Carmichael; COINTELPRO; short and long-term gains'),
  t(7, '### Key Content Review: Paper 2 Topics\n\n**Black Consciousness and 1976 Uprising:**\n- Steve Biko; SASO; BPC; psychological liberation; Soweto Uprising; 16 June 1976; Hector Pieterson; legacy\n\n**Internal Resistance 1980s:**\n- Trade unions; COSATU; UDF; Tricameral Parliament; Mass Democratic Movement; End Conscription Campaign; Black Sash; States of Emergency; BLAs\n\n**TRC:**\n- Reasons for TRC; structure (three committees); Desmond Tutu; restorative vs retributive justice; amnesty process; case studies; reparations; debates; final report\n\n**Negotiated Settlement:**\n- De Klerk\u2019s speech; Mandela\u2019s release; CODESA; Boipatong; Record of Understanding; Chris Hani\u2019s assassination; 27 April 1994; GNU\n\n**End of Cold War:**\n- Gorbachev; glasnost and perestroika; Solidarity in Poland; Fall of Berlin Wall; disintegration of USSR; impact on SA\n\n**New World Order:**\n- Unipolar to multipolar; globalisation; impact on SA'),
  t(8, '### Exam Tips for History\n\n1. **Read the question carefully**: Identify the command word (discuss, explain, evaluate, compare, to what extent) and what topic/period is being assessed\n2. **Plan your essay**: Spend 5 minutes planning before writing. Identify your main points and evidence\n3. **Use specific evidence**: Vague generalisations lose marks. Include dates, names, events, and statistics\n4. **Answer the question asked**: Do not write everything you know about a topic. Stay focused on what is being asked\n5. **Show different perspectives**: History is about interpretation. Show you understand that different people see events differently\n6. **For source-based questions**: Always refer to the source by name or number. Quote directly where possible\n7. **Time management**: You have approximately 1 mark per minute. Do not spend too long on one question\n8. **Write clearly**: Use paragraphs. Begin each paragraph with a clear point statement\n9. **Use historical terminology**: Terms like "primary source", "bias", "propaganda", "segregation", "containment" show understanding\n10. **For "to what extent" questions**: You need to weigh up both sides before giving your judgement'),
  q(9, 'In a history essay worth 50 marks, approximately how many PEEL paragraphs should the body contain?',
    ['3 to 5 paragraphs', '1 to 2 paragraphs', '7 to 10 paragraphs', 'As many as possible'], 0,
    'A well-structured 50-mark essay typically contains 3 to 5 body paragraphs, each making one clear point supported by evidence. This allows enough depth without being superficial or running out of time.'),
  fb(10, 'In Paper 1, you should allocate approximately ___ minutes per mark. The essay introduction should define key terms, provide context, and state your ___.',
    ['1', 'thesis'],
    'With 150 marks in 3 hours, you have approximately 1 minute per mark. The introduction should define key terms, provide historical context, and state your thesis (argument/line of argument) to set up the essay.'),
];

